  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1 & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(-\\frac{1}{2})={120}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${120}^{\u2218}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={120}^{\u2218}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the second quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{\u2218}.$ Since $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{\u2218}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952163/assets/ec1135cb0a4d35130a1c11baf9f421a3cdb4bc22cc1b436686dce722fa3c812e.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{\u2218}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{\u2218}+{60}^{\u2218} \\\\  & ={240}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get a second solution that's within the required domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{\u2218}$ from $X:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{\u2218} \\\\  & ={240}^{\u2218}-{360}^{\u2218} \\\\  & =-{120}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\pm {120}^{\u2218}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=120\\cdot (-120)=-14\\,400.\n$$",
